      <h2>The long tail and the OEM/private-label model</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond the majors, a large number of mid-size and small lead-acid producers operate in Hebei, Zhejiang,
        Jiangsu and Guangdong. Their business model is typically <strong>OEM and private-label supply</strong> — they
        make batteries to another brand's specification, often for export. Their products are usually interchangeable
        in specification but vary in quality control, certification and documentation. The consequence: in this long
        tail, the <strong>documentation is the differentiator</strong>, not the brand, because the batteries themselves
        look alike.
      </p>

      <h2>How to verify a claim</h2>
      <p>
        When a supplier states a factory area, production lines, certifications or CCA values, verify each against a
        source rather than taking the marketing page at face value. The key checks are listed in the
        <a href="/verification-criteria/">verification criteria</a>: the company registration, the certification
        (IATF 16949, ISO) with its issuing context, and the product-level test data (CCA per a named standard).
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> In Chinese lead-acid, the brand name matters less than the documentation — because
        the long tail makes interchangeable batteries, and the differentiator is the test data and certification a
        supplier can actually show, not the logo on the case.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The majors have scale; the long tail has price. A buyer who verifies CCA (with its
        standard), certification and batch test data can source from either confidently. A buyer who trusts the
        marketing page is gambling on claims that are cheap to make — and in OEM/private-label supply, that gamble is
        the whole game.</p>
